firefox: hold every declaration of a mapped name to the value of its row

assert-ff-agree.sh read all declarations of a custom property of the
map in a sheet as one value. A second declaration thus failed the
check, also at the value of the row. A rule for one element of the
chrome must declare such a property again when a chrome rule sets it
on that element. That chrome rule hides the value that the element
takes from :root.

Every declaration of a custom property of a row must now hold one
value in a sheet. That value must equal the value of every colour key
of the matching half. value() now prints each distinct value one time.
agree() fails a row when one sheet gives more than one value for a
name of the row. This check also covers a row with no colour key, for
example the menuBorder row.

Three controls run over copies of the distinct dark sheet. A copy that
declares --panel-background-color again at the value of the sheet must
pass. A copy that declares it again at #141618 must fail. A copy that
sets the first declaration to #141618 must also fail. Before the
controls, the run checks that the value of the sheet is not #141618.
The build prints one line for each control.

firefox: colour the selected tab label of Firefox 157

Firefox 157 renames the custom property of the label colour of the
selected tab. The label rule of 157 reads --tab-text-color-selected,
and no rule of 157 reads --tab-selected-textcolor. The sheet wrote the
old name alone. With the theme add-on, the label of 157 thus took the
colour of the static theme. Without the add-on and with the native
theme off, it took a default of the browser. With the native theme on,
the default of the browser reads --toolbar-text-color, which the sheet
sets to the colour of the old name.

The windowText row of the map now holds the new name after the old
name. The sheet thus sets both names to the colour of the row. Firefox
156 reads the old name alone, so the old name stays.

The pin of the properties holds the new name. Its header now names
Firefox 155 and Firefox 157 as the sources of the pinned names. The
floor of the name check becomes 37, which mkfloors.sh prints for this
tree. The manifest does not change, because the colour keys of the row
do not change.

firefox: pin the accent stops of the selected tab to the highlight

Firefox 157 sets browser.nova.enabled to true by default. Under that
preference, the accent of the selected tab is a violet to orange
gradient of two custom properties: --tab-border-color-selected-leading
and --tab-border-color-selected-trailing. The separator under the
account block of the app menu paints that gradient, with and without
the theme add-on. Without the add-on and with the native theme off,
the border of the selected tab paints it too. The sheet gave neither
property a colour of the scheme.

The highlight row of the map now holds the two names. The sheet thus
sets both stops to the highlight colour, and each gradient becomes one
solid line of that colour. assert-ff-agree.sh binds every name of a
row to the colour keys of that row, so it checks the two new lines
with no change. The floor of the name check becomes 39, which
mkfloors.sh prints for this tree.

firefox: pin the primary accent to the highlight ladder

With the native theme on, the browser takes --color-accent-primary
from the system colour AccentColor and --button-text-color-primary
from AccentColorText. On the measured GTK desktop, AccentColor held the
highlight text colour of the scheme and AccentColorText held the
highlight. The Sign in button of the app menu thus painted a near black
ground. The focus ring of the sidebar search reads the same token, and
it drew near black on the dark sidebar.

The sheet now pins --color-accent-primary to the highlight colour and
--button-text-color-primary to the highlight text colour. The two names
join the highlight row and the highlightText row of the map.
assert-ff-agree.sh holds all names of a row to one colour, so the hover
and the active names take two new rows. Their new roles read the
highlight ladder at the steps of the hover ground and the pressed
ground of a button.

The new rows carry no colour key, so the manifest does not change. The
floor of the name check becomes 43, which mkfloors.sh prints for this
tree. The floor of the rows becomes 20, the row count of the built map.

firefox: follow the round key for the radius tokens of the chrome

Firefox 157 sets browser.nova.enabled to true by default. Under that
preference, the radius tokens of tabs, buttons and toolbar buttons are
24px, so these widgets render as pills. Without it they are 8px, on
Firefox 156 and 157. Neither value follows the round key of QtCurve.

The sheet now holds one more :root block after the icon rules. It pins
nine radius tokens of the chrome to the radius that Gtk::radius() gives
under the round key. Each token takes one widget kind: a tab, a button,
a menu as a window, or a menu item. Each kind takes the size of a radius
call of the GTK writer for that kind. Under round=extra, the tokens of
tabs, buttons and menus take 4.5px, and the tokens of menu items take
2.5px. The same pins apply under Firefox 156.

The block pins no --menuitem-border-radius, because every menupopup and
panel declares that token again, so a pin at the root moves nothing. The
square flags of popup menus and tooltips map to no token. Gtk::radius()
reads neither flag, and --popup-border-radius also sets the corners of
the urlbar, which is an entry. The manifest of the static theme carries
no radius, so it does not change.

The contract of buildSheet() now names the radius block and the scroll
bar rule of the sidebars. The floor of the name check becomes 52, which
mkfloors.sh prints for this tree.

firefox: hold the form popups to the popup colours of the sheet

Firefox 157 gives the autocomplete popup of a form and the date and
time picker the colour scheme of the page. Rules of the browser set
--panel-background-color to Field on these two panels, and Field
follows that scheme. A light page under a dark sheet thus gave both
panels a white ground. Firefox 156 gave them the scheme of the content
preference.

The sheet now holds two rules after the popup shadow rule. The first
rule declares the popup background, text and border of the map again
on both panels, at the values of the menu rows. It also pins
color-scheme to dark when the luminance of the window background is
below 0.5, and to light when it is not. A probe showed that the three
colour pins alone do not hold. The picker paints a Field body of its
own, and the autocomplete rows keep the text of a light scheme.

The rows take the colour of their list. A rule of the toolkit sets the
colour of that list to FieldText. That rule does not read
--panel-text-color. FieldText follows the pinned scheme and can differ
from the popup text. The second rule sets the colour of the list to
var(--panel-text-color), the text pin of the first rule. The sheet thus
holds the autocomplete rows to the pinned popup text of both schemes.

assert-ff-agree.sh accepts the repeated declarations of the first
rule, because each one holds the value of its row. The rules add no
name to the pin, because they declare no custom property outside the
map.

The changes sit on the branch work/firefox-theme-ff157 of the local
tree, as commits 15 to 20 of 20. The branch holds those twenty commits
above the head of work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on
system/qtcurve holds. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme.
Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

